       "24x24": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ddfaabcc9635974ebed182e89941a947?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FSB-2.png",
       "32x32": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ddfaabcc9635974ebed182e89941a947?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FSB-2.png",
       "48x48": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/ddfaabcc9635974ebed182e89941a947?d=https%3A%2F%2Favatar-management--avatars.us-west-2.prod.public.atl-paas.net%2Finitials%2FSB-2.png"
      },
      "displayName": "Stephen Bayliss",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=6089a4cb543264006867626e",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p>We can dispense with transactions altogether, so long as we have single-threaded updates.</p>\n\n<p>Implemented this, it means catching errors on reads, and retrying operations (ie doing what dbxml deadlock detection would do, but a simpler version).  </p>\n\n<p>(there would be a more elegant way of doing this, by blocking read threads if a write is in progress and vice-versa, but allowing multi-thread reading but only single-thread writing.  to be revisited as part of \n    <span class=\"jira-issue-macro resolved\" data-jira-key=\"FCREPO-576\" >\n                <a href=\"https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/browse/FCREPO-576\" class=\"jira-issue-macro-key issue-link\"  title=\"Remove dbxml or make it an optional dependency\" >\n            <img class=\"icon\" src=\"https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/2/universal_avatar/view/type/issuetype/avatar/10315?size=medium\" />\n            FCREPO-576\n        </a>\n                                                    <span class=\"aui-lozenge aui-lozenge-subtle aui-lozenge-success jira-macro-single-issue-export-pdf\">Closed</span>\n            </span>\n, added a note to that issue to cover this)</p>\n\n<p>Load-tested this with 10 concurrent update threads, 50 concurrent write threads, update threads each perform updates (adds, reads, deletes) to a set of 200 policy objects - no errors.</p>",
     "created": "12/Jan/11 12:03 AM",
